From 558a0067aec4b879ddd48a9bc33d0b0d5220e50f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EmberGate <embergate@localhost>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:58:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] llama : optional <arch>.layer_schedule for synthetic layer
 repeats (EmberGate R5/S1)

A GGUF may carry <arch>.layer_schedule, an array of FILE trunk layer indices, one per trunk
POSITION (e.g. B1 on a 32-layer Qwen3.5 = 0..7, 4..7, 8..31 = 36 positions). The trunk then runs
those layers in that order with the weights shared (no copies): tensors are created once per file
layer under their file names, and layers[p] is a copy of the layer struct of sched[p].

Every per-layer hparams array is re-indexed by position (n_layer_all = len + n_layer_nextn), so each
position is its own layer index for every cache (KV for full attention, conv + recurrent state for
DeltaNet: a repeated recurrent pass needs its own state slot). MTP/NextN layers keep their file names
and sit after the trunk. dev_layer is remapped the same way, so a position's cache lives with its
weights. hparams.n_layer_file keeps the file's trunk count.

Generic (llama_model_base), no per-arch code: hparams are swapped to the file view while
load_arch_tensors + the generic scale pass create tensors, then expanded to positions before
done_getting_tensors(). Refused (with the reason) for archs whose layer-index scalars a schedule
would not remap (n_layer_dense_lead, n_layer_kv_from_start, router_layer).

Absent key: nothing changes (CPU: plain GGUF logits byte-identical to the unpatched build).
Not handled: llama-model-saver writes the position count as block_count.
